window.docContents[42].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fundamental-solutions-and-classical-harmonic-functions--Maximum-principles-and-energy-identities":{"contents":"The weak maximum and minimum principles, comparison, and Dirichlet uniqueness\nare proved for Euclidean balls and functions continuous on the closed ball.\nThe strong maximum and minimum principles are proved for harmonic functions on\narbitrary open preconnected sets.\n\nThe variable-coefficient theory treats\n. Its weak maximum\nprinciple assumes an open domain with compact closure, nonnegative principal\nquadratic forms, a uniform positive lower bound on one diagonal entry, an\nupper bound on the corresponding drift component, and .\nA boundary bound  with  controls a classical subsolution\ncontinuous on the closure.\n\nThe strong principle uses local uniform ellipticity and local upper bounds\non the trace, drift norm, and nonnegative potential. An attained maximum\n with  forces constancy on an open preconnected domain.\nBoth matrix principles allow arbitrary real principal matrices: their\nself-adjoint parts give the same operator on  functions.\nCoefficient continuity is not required.\n\nThe Hopf lemma for the same operators gives a strictly positive outer normal\nderivative at a boundary maximum point with an interior ball condition. A\nHarnack inequality is proved for nonnegative  solutions of\n with continuous bounded coefficients, symmetric uniformly elliptic\nprincipal part, and a potential of either sign, through a contact-set measure\nestimate and geometric decay of superlevel sets.\nRadial symmetry of positive solutions of  on the unit ball\nwith zero boundary values is proved by moving planes, for  solutions\nand globally Lipschitz .\n\nGreen's first and second identities are proved on coordinate boxes with their\nboundary fluxes expanded face by face. The resulting classical Dirichlet\nuniqueness theorem assumes pointwise  data on a nondegenerate closed\nbox. This is separate from weak uniqueness in , where the boundary\ncondition and integration by parts are encoded variationally. On a bounded open\nset with  graph boundary and forcing continuous in , the\nclassical Dirichlet principle\nidentifies the solutions of  in the classical boundary class with\nthe minimizers of the Dirichlet energy over its finite-energy members.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Fundamental solutions and classical harmonic functions","header":"5.7.1.1.3. Maximum principles and energy identities","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fundamental-solutions-and-classical-harmonic-functions--Maximum-principles-and-energy-identities"},"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Interior-and-Boundary-Regularity--Operational-Boundary-Geometry":{"contents":"Boundary regularity needs more than an abstract smooth-boundary label: the\nproof differentiates flattening maps, transfers weak derivatives, and sums\nlocalized estimates through a finite partition of unity. The operational\natlases record exactly that data, with concrete instances for the\ninterval and every positive-dimensional Euclidean unit ball.\n\nThis reuses the geometry of\nSobolev extension, but asks it to do more.\nExtension continues a function whose first derivatives are already controlled.\nBoundary regularity must obtain an additional derivative from the equation.\nFlattening transforms the coefficients as well as the function, and\ndifferentiating that transformed equation requires extra chart derivatives.\nWe use explicit atlases so that this extra cost is part of the\nhypotheses, even on the familiar interval and ball.\n\nA  boundary atlas consists of a finite extension atlas whose flattening\ncharts and inverse charts are twice continuously differentiable.\n\nThe concrete atlases follow Evans's boundary-flattening argument on\npp. 334–340 and start from a finite cover of the boundary.\n\nA finite boundary cover consists of bounded open neighborhoods  whose\nunion contains the frontier  of a bounded open domain.\nAdding  as the interior member gives a finite open cover of\n. Mathlib's smooth partition-of-unity theorem then\ncanonically supplies compactly supported interior and boundary test functions\nsubordinate to those sets and summing to one on .\n\nA cover alone carries no analytic information about the boundary. The\nflattening charts add it: each boundary patch receives a map identifying the\ndomain locally with the upper half-space, and the differentiability class of\nthese maps is what distinguishes the  extension atlas from the \nregularity atlas.\n\nAfter the subordinate cutoffs are fixed, one attaches a flattening chart to\neach boundary neighborhood. Only the compact support of the corresponding\ncutoff must lie in the chart source. Globally represented  charts\nproduce an extension atlas, while  charts produce the operational\nboundary atlas used by elliptic regularity.\n\nThe first instance is the interval. In one dimension the boundary consists\nof two points, and affine maps flatten it.\n\nFor , the affine endpoint maps\n and  identify neighborhoods of the two\nboundary points with neighborhoods in the upper half-line .\nBoth maps are affine with affine inverses, hence smooth to every finite\norder. Together with an interior patch and a finite subordinate\npartition of unity, they furnish the operational  boundary atlas used\nby the global elliptic and parabolic regularity theorems.\n\nIn higher dimensions the unit ball is the model domain, and its atlas is\nassembled in two stages. The first stage is the cover: finitely many signed\ncoordinate caps containing the sphere, together with a subordinate partition\nof unity; each cap is later flattened by a graph chart.\n\nIn every positive dimension, the Euclidean unit ball has a finite cover by\n signed coordinate caps together with a smooth subordinate partition of\nunity. The interior and boundary cutoffs have compact support in their\nassigned sets and sum exactly to one on the ball. Its global defining\nfunction  is smooth, is positive exactly in the\nball, and has nonzero radial differential on the unit sphere.The caps cover the sphere: a point of the unit sphere is nonzero, so at least\none coordinate is either positive or negative, and the identity\n shows that the remaining tangential coordinates have\nsquared norm strictly less than one, placing the point in the corresponding\nsigned cap. Together with the open ball, these finitely many caps cover the\nclosed ball. Mathlib's smooth partition-of-unity theorem supplies the cutoffs,\nand boundedness of the cover sets makes their supports compact.\n\nOn each cap the sphere is the graph of a height function over the tangential\ncoordinates, and subtracting or adding that height in the normal coordinate\nflattens the cap. The constructor requires chart representatives\ndifferentiable on all of , so the local height\n is replaced by a globally smooth function\nagreeing with it on a slightly smaller tangential cylinder.\n\nFor a globally smooth height , the triangular\nmapsflatten the region below the graph of  and the region above the graph\nof , respectively, to the standard upper half-space. Their forward and\ninverse maps retain every finite differentiability order, and restriction to\na bounded open source gives the bounded partial charts required by the\noperational atlas. On every smaller\ntangential cylinder of the unit sphere, we also construct a global\n height agreeing with .\n\nThe flattening maps preserve every tangential coordinate and replace the\nnormal coordinate by  or . Direct coordinate\ncalculations give their inverses. The inequalities  and\n, respectively, are equivalent to positivity of the flattened\nnormal coordinate. Componentwise\ncalculus proves the required differentiability. Restricting each global\nhomeomorphism to a bounded open source gives a bounded partial chart because\nthe image of the compact source closure is bounded. For the sphere, the local\nheight  is smooth on the region where its\nradicand is positive; Mathlib's relative smooth-extension theorem produces a\nglobally smooth function agreeing with it on any strictly smaller closed\ntangential cylinder.\n\nFeeding the cap cover and the graph charts into the constructor gives the\nconcrete  atlas on which the global regularity\ntheorems can be instantiated for the unit ball in every positive dimension.\n\nFor every , quantitative signed caps, coordinate permutations,\nglobally smooth extensions of\n, and triangular graph-flattening maps\nassemble into a finite operational  boundary atlas for\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Elliptic Regularity and Solution Operators\u0009Interior and Boundary Regularity","header":"5.6.1.1. Operational Boundary Geometry","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Interior-and-Boundary-Regularity--Operational-Boundary-Geometry"},"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Green-Kernels-and-Poisson-Representation--The-Ball-Kernel-and-Dirichlet-Problem":{"contents":"Kelvin inversion places an image pole outside the ball, so subtracting the\nimage fundamental solution produces a kernel that is harmonic away from the\nphysical pole and vanishes on the sphere. The negative outward normal\nderivative  is the Poisson kernel, and the\nassociated Poisson extension is regular, harmonic, recovers its boundary\nvalues, and is the unique such solution.\n\nTo make the construction concrete, consider the three-dimensional unit ball.\nThe fundamental solution is . For an interior pole\n with , the Kelvin image is , which lies\noutside the ball since . The Green's function is then\n\n\n\nOn the boundary sphere , the identity  makes\nthe two terms cancel, confirming . The outward normal derivative\nof  is negative; its negative gives the Poisson kernel\n\n\n\nThis explicit formula shows that the Poisson kernel is positive, singular as\n, and has mass one over the sphere. For the ball of\nradius , scaling replaces  by  and introduces the\nappropriate dimensional normalization (Evans, 2010, pp. 40–41;\n, Theorem 2.6).\n\nIn positive dimension, write  when .\nThe unit-ball Green kernel is the total functionwith the continuous  branch written explicitly in Lean. The first\nvariable is the physical pole and the second is the field point; in the\none-dimensional branch,  and  denote their sole coordinates. For\n the kernel is the Kelvin construction of Evans §2.2.4; for  it\nis the Dirichlet kernel of  on .\n\nThe defining property of a Green kernel is that it vanishes when the field point\nreaches the boundary. For the Kelvin construction this is the inversion identity\n on the unit sphere, which\ngives the two fundamental-solution terms arguments of equal norm.\n\nFor , an interior pole and a boundary field point satisfy\n\nWrite  for the Kelvin image of a nonzero pole.\nFor , the inversion identity givesThus the physical and image arguments of the fundamental solution have the\nsame norm, so the two terms in the Green kernel cancel. The proof treats\ndimension two and dimensions at least three separately because the\nfundamental solution is logarithmic in the former and a negative power in the\nlatter. If , both formulas reduce directly using\n.\n\nBoundary vanishing concerns the sphere; harmonicity concerns the interior. For a\nnonzero interior pole the image  lies outside the closed ball, so both\nfundamental-solution terms are harmonic in  throughout\n. Harmonicity off the pole together with vanishing on\nthe sphere makes  a Green kernel.\n\nFor , , and , the field-variable\nLaplacian vanishes:\n\nExpand the Kelvin-inversion formula.  Each fundamental-solution term is\nharmonic away from its pole, and the geometric hypotheses keep both relevant\npoles outside the point where the field-variable Laplacian is evaluated.\n\nThe Poisson kernel is the negative outward normal derivative\n on the sphere, in which the two\nfundamental-solution terms combine into a single closed expression. We take\nthat closed form, scaled to a ball of radius , as the definition of the\nkernel, following Evans's computation of ; the theorems\nabout the Poisson extension work from the formula directly.\n\nFor a positive radius , the ball Poisson kernel isIts boundary-value interpretation uses  and\n; the Lean definition is total.\n\nIntegrating the kernel against boundary data over the sphere gives the Poisson\nextension. The definition pulls the -independent prefactor\n outside the integral, leaving\n as the integrand; this is the form in which the\nextension is differentiated.\n\nThe Poisson extension integrates the prescribed boundary datum over the\nradius- sphere with the normalization used by the ball Poisson kernel.\nAnalytic theorems about this total function supply positivity of ,\ncontinuity of the data, and the appropriate interior or boundary hypotheses.\n\nThe prefactor form and the kernel form of the extension are equal as total\nfunctions, with no hypotheses on , , or , so later theorems may pass\nbetween them freely.\n\nThe prefactor form of the ball extension is algebraically equal to the sphere\nintegral of .\n\nInsert the explicit formula for  into the sphere integral. The\nfactor  is independent of  and\ncan be pulled outside, leaving exactly the prefactor definition of the\nPoisson extension.\n\nThe Poisson extension is meaningful only inside the ball. The Dirichlet\ncandidate glues it to the datum on the sphere and outside, and attaching the\ndata definitionally rather than through a limit is what lets boundary equality\nbe exact and uniqueness be stated as an equation on the closed ball.\n\nThe boundary-attached Dirichlet candidate is the total functionThus it is the Poisson extension in the open ball and agrees definitionally\nwith the prescribed ambient datum outside it.\n\nClassical harmonicity requires two derivatives, but the same kernel gives every\nderivative order in the interior. Near a fixed interior point, a smooth cutoff\nremoves the inverse-distance singularity without changing the sphere integral.\nMathlib's convolution regularity theorem then gives smoothness.\n\nIn positive dimension, for a positive radius and a continuous ambient\nrepresentative of the boundary datum, differentiation under the sphere\nintegral gives a  extension on the open ball. The linked theorems\nallow every regularity order , both at individual\ninterior points and throughout the ball. The choice  supplies the\nregularity needed by the classical Laplacian statement.\n\nFix an interior point and choose a smaller neighborhood whose distance from\nthe boundary sphere is positive. The differences between points in this\nneighborhood and points on the sphere lie in a compact annulus away from zero.\nReplace the inverse-distance kernel by a smooth compactly supported kernel\nthat agrees on this annulus. Its convolution with the boundary datum is\nsmooth and agrees locally with the original potential. Multiplying by the\nsmooth Poisson prefactor preserves every derivative order.\n\nGiven the two derivatives, the Laplacian of the extension is the sphere integral\nof , and for  the kernel is harmonic in\n. The theorem carries this pointwise cancellation through the integral.\n\nIn positive dimension, if ,  is\ncontinuous, and , then the coordinate Laplacian of the Poisson\nextension vanishes:The proof differentiates the kernel twice, uses the common norm-square\nidentities, and performs the resulting sphere-integral cancellation.\n\nUse the two derivative formulas justified by interior regularity and sum their\ndiagonal terms. After writing all denominators over\n, the norm-square identities for boundary points\n make the numerator cancel. Integrating the zero kernel\nLaplacian yields .\n\nInterior regularity and the vanishing Laplacian are exactly the two clauses of\nthe harmonicity predicate. Combining them for the boundary-attached candidate,\nwhich agrees with  on the open ball, gives the existence half of the\nDirichlet theorem at the  level.\n\nIn positive dimension, for  and continuous ambient data , the\nboundary-attached solution satisfiesInterior  regularity holds as well, by the\nsmoothness theorem.\n\nInside the ball, the boundary-attached solution is definitionally the Poisson\nextension. Apply its interior  theorem and its pointwise Laplacian-zero\ntheorem; together these are exactly the classical harmonicity predicate on\n.\n\nHarmonicity inside the ball settles nothing about the boundary condition.\nBoundary recovery rests on the kernel being positive with mass one and\nconcentrating at  as , so that the Poisson\nintegral of continuous data converges to .\n\nIn positive dimension, if  and  is continuous, thenThe proof first shows\n as  through  for every\n; the attached total solution then gives exact\nboundary equality.\n\nSubtract  inside the Poisson integral and use that the kernel has\nmass one. Continuity makes this difference uniformly small near ,\nwhile positivity and concentration of the kernel control the complementary\npart. This proves the interior limit; the piecewise definition supplies exact\nboundary values and glues the two continuous pieces on the closed ball.\n\nUniqueness comes from the maximum principle. The difference of two solutions\nis harmonic in the ball, continuous on its closure, and zero on the sphere, and\ntwo applications of the weak maximum principle force it to vanish. The\nmean-value property and the derivative estimates use this theorem to identify\na harmonic function with its Poisson extension.\n\nIn positive dimension, let , let  be continuous, and let\n. Ifthen\n\nSubtract the two harmonic functions.  Their difference is continuous up to\nthe boundary and vanishes there, so the weak maximum principle applied to the\ndifference and its negative forces it to vanish on the ball.\n\nEvans states these properties of  together as Poisson's formula for the\nball.\n\nIn positive dimension, for  and continuous , the Poisson formula\ngives a field  such thatfor every .\n\nThe three clauses are the interior Laplacian identity, interior \nregularity, and pointwise boundary convergence of the Poisson integral.\n\nWith uniqueness, the Poisson formula gives the only solution of the ball\nDirichlet problem with continuous data.\nFor model domains with explicit geometry, the classical and\npotential-theoretic routes therefore give the same answer.\nFor general domains, uniqueness instead follows from the maximum principle\nalone, without an explicit representation\n(, Theorem 2.5).\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Model Dirichlet Problems and Harmonic Rigidity\u0009Green Kernels and Poisson Representation","header":"5.3.1.1. The Ball Kernel and Dirichlet Problem","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Green-Kernels-and-Poisson-Representation--The-Ball-Kernel-and-Dirichlet-Problem"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Bounds-and-Models--One-Calculation___-End-to-End":{"contents":"The derivative request\n\n-- Retain the nonzero-domain condition\n-- for log in the report.\n#cas [derive x] (log x)\n\n\n#cas [derive x]\nDomain: x ∈ ℝ\nInput (scalar ℝ): (log x)\nOutput (scalar ℝ): 1/x\nConditions: x ≠ 0\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nillustrates the complete exact path. The parser first turns the ordinary\nnotation into an expression tree. Inspecting the logarithm records a nonzero\ncondition on its argument. The differentiator constructs the candidate ,\nand the analytic differentiation theorem justifies the ordinary statement\nthat the derivative of  at  is , provided .\n\nThe condition printed by the inspection command is part of the answer.\nTo use the derivative at a particular point, a theorem-facing tactic must\nprove that condition from the hypotheses. This is the same obligation that\nappeared in the affine Burgers denominator and in the logarithmic\nfundamental solution away from its pole. Polynomial examples need no such\nexclusion, but they pass through the same proof interface.\n\nA CEGA query follows the same architecture but ends in a quantitative\ncertificate. For\n\n-- Exercise the full parser-to-certificate\n-- pipeline.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas [enclose x in [-1 / 10, 1 / 10]] (cos x)\n\n\n#cas [enclose x in [-1 / 10, 1 / 10]]\nDomain: x ∈ [-1/10, 1/10] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (cos x)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [0.995004, 1]\nUpper bound: 1\nAbsolute bound: 1\nCenter and radius: ≈0.997502 ± 0.002498\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nthe parser again produces an expression tree, and a rational interval\nevaluator constructs candidate endpoints. A successful computation returns\nthose endpoints together with a proof that every value on the input interval\nlies between them.\n\nTaylor models use enclosures to bound their remainders while retaining\npolynomial structure. Derivative bounds also help determine a sufficient\nsubdivision for quadrature.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Certified Bounds and Models","header":"10.7.1. One Calculation, End to End","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Bounds-and-Models--One-Calculation___-End-to-End"}});